          "payload": "node.web.detail"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;background:#121a31;color:#f8f5ff;overflow:auto;font-family:system-ui'><main style='padding:clamp(22px,5cqw,52px);max-width:1100px;margin:auto'><div style='border-top:3px solid #e8ce90;padding-top:18px;color:#e8ce90;font:800 12px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>THE TWILITE ZONE / LIBRARY</div><h1 style='font:600 clamp(48px,10cqw,90px)/.98 Georgia,serif;margin:30px 0 14px;letter-spacing:-.045em'>The Glossary</h1><p style='font:400 clamp(18px,2.8cqw,23px)/1.48 system-ui;max-width:740px;color:#cbd3e5'>Understand the graph. Author the graph. Build with the graph. The vocabulary grows with what you're trying to do.</p><nav style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;margin:28px 0 42px'><a href='#glossary-reading' style='color:#b8d8ff;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #b8d8ff77;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>READING</a><a href='#glossary-making' style='color:#e8ce90;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #e8ce9077;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>MAKING</a><a href='#glossary-building' style='color:#c9b8ff;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #c9b8ff77;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>BUILDING</a><a href='#glossary-patterns' style='color:#b4e9d4;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #b4e9d477;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>PATTERNS</a><a href='#glossary-in-the-app' style='color:#f1c4d5;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #f1c4d577;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>NAVIGATION</a><a href='#glossary-retired' style='color:#d0d4e3;padding:9px 13px;border:1px solid #d0d4e377;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font:700 12px system-ui'>OLDER WORDS</a></nav><div style='border-left:4px solid #e8ce90;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:30px;background:#222b46;font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#e5e5f0'>A graph is both an artifact you can open and, from outside, a node among other nodes. That shift of perspective is the key to reading Twilite.</div><section id='glossary-reading' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#b8d8ff;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>01 / READING</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>A visitor&#39;s vocabulary</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>These are the only words a visitor needs to read anything in the Twilite Zone.<p><strong>Graph or node.</strong> The same thing is a graph or a node depending on where you stand. Inside it, it is a graph, with nodes of its own. From outside, it is a node: it has faces and a Glyph, and it sits in the workspace or behind a Portal like any other node. Use whichever word fits where the reader is standing.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#b8d8ff'><strong>graph</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A thing you can open: a set of nodes and the edges between them, saved as one <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>.node</code> file with its own address (<code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>tlz://host/path/root.node</code>).</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#b8d8ff'><strong>node</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>One thing inside a graph. Stored as an entry in <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>nodes</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#b8d8ff'><strong>edge</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A named connection between two nodes. Its name says what the connection means; click the name to read about that kind of connection. Stored as an entry in <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>edges</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#b8d8ff'><strong>page</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>What a graph or a node looks like when you are close enough to read it: its first, largest face.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#b8d8ff'><strong>Portal</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A door to another graph. Click it to go through. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>portal</code>.</div></div></div></section><section id='glossary-making' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#e8ce90;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>02 / MAKING</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>How graphs become things</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>Words that arrive when someone starts making things.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>content</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The words, pictures or markup a node shows. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>content</code>, with <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.content.kind</code> and <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.content.value</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>link</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>An edge on the web: a connection to a graph or a web page, written in content or set on a node (<code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.link</code>, <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>url</code>, <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>href</code>). It is drawn as an edge, by the <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>link</code> edge class, wherever both of its ends are in the workspace.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>ground</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>What you stand on inside a graph: where you land when you go in. A graph with graph content has one; a document has none. Joined to the root by a <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>ground</code> edge from the root&#39;s <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>ground</code> port.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>faces</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>How a graph or node looks from further away, largest to smallest. The author names them; the usual names are Detail, Summary and Icon. The first face is the page. What the app shows depends on a face&#39;s place in the order and the room it has, never on its name. The order ends at the Glyph. Stored as <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>face</code> nodes joined by <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>next-face</code> edges.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>Glyph</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A node&#39;s mark: the symbol that stands for it. Faces are how a node looks; the Glyph is how it is recognised. A graph has one because, seen from outside, a graph is a node. It is shown when there is no room for even the smallest face, and wherever the node is named in a small space: lists, the trail, link chips, tabs, bookmarks. It has its own editor and its name is always Glyph. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>glyph</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>template</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A graph you start a new graph from. Templates are how the shapes that work get passed on, and each generation built from one can become a template in turn.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#e8ce90'><strong>workspace</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>Where open graphs sit side by side. Not stored in graphs.</div></div></div></section><section id='glossary-building' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#c9b8ff;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>03 / BUILDING</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>The authoring language</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>Words for the template builder. A visitor never needs them.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>root</strong> (noun)</div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The node a graph begins from. It says what the graph is (its name, description and faces) and everything else in the graph hangs from it. Every graph has exactly one. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>root</code>, in the file <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>root.node</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>root</strong> (verb)</div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>To select several graphs in the workspace and make a new graph from them, with a root of its own. It is what Twilite has instead of grouping. It works on graphs only: lifting nodes out of a graph into a new one is copy and paste. <em>Not built yet.</em></div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Surface</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>One ordered set of faces a graph shows from outside. A graph can have more than one. A <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>surface</code> edge from the root to the first face.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Port</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A named place where a graph offers something to other graphs. The root is the graph&#39;s root Port. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>port</code>; an edge&#39;s ends are its <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>sourcePort</code> and <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>targetPort</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>export</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>What a graph offers to other graphs: a class, a set of classes, or the whole graph. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>export</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>import</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>What a graph uses from another graph&#39;s export. A class used in a graph must come in through an import. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>import</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Bridge</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The connection from an export to the import that uses it: one graph drawing on another. A classed edge (the <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>bridge</code> edge class), drawn in the workspace between the two graphs, and what Tidy arranges by. Not stored: worked out from the imports and exports of open graphs.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>class</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The definition a node or edge follows: which Properties it has and how it looks. On nodes and edges alike, <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>type</code> is the class key and <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>classRef</code> the class graph&#39;s address.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Edge Class</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A class for edges: what a kind of connection means and what it may join. A graph built from the Edge Class template.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Property</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>One named value on a node, as its class defines it. Every Property announces <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>property.changed</code> when its value changes; nothing happens unless an edge is drawn from it. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>property</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Method</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The only code in a graph. It runs when something invokes it, and declares what it may change; the app refuses anything else. A Method has an <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>invokes</code> port of its own, so one Method can run the next. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>method</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>invokes</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The edge that makes something happen: from what raises an event (a Property, a node, a button, a Method) to the Method it runs, naming the event. Every source draws its own edge: structure matters. Edge type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>invokes</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Membrane</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A graph&#39;s boundary: what it holds in, and where its ports sit for things to cross. A graph grows from its root and is held by its membrane. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>membrane</code> for its control points, edge <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>membrane</code>, and the root&#39;s <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.membrane</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>Specification</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The page that defines a primitive. An ordinary graph under <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>library/primitives/</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#c9b8ff'><strong>primitive</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>A canonical part of the graph model. See the Primitives index for the current inventory.</div></div></div></section><section id='glossary-patterns' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#b4e9d4;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>04 / PATTERNS</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>Shapes, not categories</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>A graph can grow into anything. Some shapes come up again and again: a page on its own, a picture, a list of doors to other graphs. People call these <strong>documents</strong>, <strong>scenes</strong> and <strong>collections</strong>. They are patterns, not kinds of graph: nothing in a file says which one a graph is, and a graph can be more than one at once. What a graph does follows from what is in it, never from a label on it (a graph with no ground shows its page because it has no ground). Templates are how the patterns are passed on.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'></div><p style='font:400 16px/1.6 system-ui;color:#d5d9e9'>Documents, scenes and collections describe recurring <em>patterns</em>. They are not separate graph file types.</p></section><section id='glossary-in-the-app' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#f1c4d5;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>05 / NAVIGATION</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>Finding your way</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>Words for finding your way.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#f1c4d5'><strong>inside</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>Where you are after entering a graph: standing on its ground.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#f1c4d5'><strong>trail</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>The path at the top of the window: Workspace › graph › node › Inside.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#f1c4d5'><strong>going through</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>Following a Portal, or the end of a line, to where it leads.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#f1c4d5'><strong>Tidy</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>Arranging the workspace: graphs joined by a Bridge close together (imports on the left, exports on the right), Portal graphs further out.</div></div></div></section><section id='glossary-retired' style='padding:34px 0;border-top:1px solid #48516c'><div style='color:#d0d4e3;font:800 13px system-ui;letter-spacing:.18em'>06 / OLDER WORDS</div><h2 style='font:600 clamp(28px,5cqw,43px)/1.07 Georgia,serif;margin:10px 0 15px'>What terminology replaced</h2><p style='font:400 16px/1.65 system-ui;color:#d2d6e6;margin:0 0 18px'>Words older graphs and pages use, and what replaced them. A current graph never uses them.</p><div style='display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(235px,1fr));gap:0 30px'><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Declaration</strong>, <strong>manifest</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>the root.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>View</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>a face. <strong>View sequence</strong> — next-face.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>landing surface</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>the ground.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Field</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>a Property.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Markdown node</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>Content with kind <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>markdown</code>.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Bridge node</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>an import or an export. The Bridge is the connection between them.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Graph Reference</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>a Portal.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>groups</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>rooting.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>frame</strong>, <strong>border</strong>, <strong>wrap</strong> (for a graph&#39;s boundary)</div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>the Membrane.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>handle</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>An editor interaction point on a node. A Port is a named connection interface; the modern model distinguishes ports from handles.</div></div><div style='padding:11px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #3c4968'><div style='font:700 14px/1.35 system-ui;color:#d0d4e3'><strong>Event Handler</strong>, <strong>Script</strong></div><div style='font:400 13px/1.55 system-ui;color:#c7d0e5;margin-top:4px'>leaving in favour of Methods and the <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>invokes</code> edge.</div></div></div></section><footer style='display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:24px;border-top:2px solid #e8ce90;padding:26px 0 50px;font:700 15px system-ui'><a style='color:#e8ce90' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/root.node'>← The Library</a><a style='color:#e8ce90' href='tlz://twilite.zone/library/primitives/root.node'>Explore the Primitives</a><a style='color:#e8ce90' href='tlz://twilite.zone/boulevard/root.node'>The Boulevard</a></footer></main></div>"
        },
        "memo": ""
      }
    },
