        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#222b49,#171f38);border:2px solid #e8ce9088;border-radius:18px;padding:5cqw;color:#f8f3ff;font-family:system-ui'><div style='color:#e8ce90;font:800 3.4cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.16em'>02 / MAKING</div><h2 style='margin:3cqw 0 2cqw;font:600 8cqw/.98 Georgia,serif'>How graphs become things</h2><p style='margin:0 0 3cqw;font:400 3.2cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#bfcce7'>What you meet when you begin authoring.</p><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#e8ce90;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>content</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>The words, pictures or markup a node shows. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>content</code>, with <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.content.kind</code> and <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.content.value</code>.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#e8ce90;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>link</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>An edge on the web: a connection to a graph or a web page, written in content or set on a node (<code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>data.link</code>, <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>url</code>, <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>href</code>). It is drawn as an edge, by the <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>link</code> edge class, wherever both of its ends are in the workspace.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#e8ce90;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>ground</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>What you stand on inside a graph: where you land when you go in. A graph with graph content has one; a document has none. Joined to the root by a <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>ground</code> edge from the root&#39;s <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>ground</code> port.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#e8ce90;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>faces</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>How a graph or node looks from further away, largest to smallest. The author names them; the usual names are Detail, Summary and Icon. The first face is the page. What the app shows depends on a face&#39;s place in the order and the room it has, never on its name. The order ends at the Glyph. Stored as <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>face</code> nodes joined by <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>next-face</code> edges.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#e8ce90;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Glyph</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>A node&#39;s mark: the symbol that stands for it. Faces are how a node looks; the Glyph is how it is recognised. A graph has one because, seen from outside, a graph is a node. It is shown when there is no room for even the smallest face, and wherever the node is named in a small space: lists, the trail, link chips, tabs, bookmarks. It has its own editor and its name is always Glyph. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>glyph</code>.</span></div><div style='margin-top:15px;color:#e8ce90;font:700 13px system-ui'>+ 2 more terms in the full Glossary page →</div></div>"
        },
        "sourceMarkdown": "Words that arrive when someone starts making things.\n\n- **content** — The words, pictures or markup a node shows. Node type `content`, with `data.content.kind` and `data.content.value`.\n- **link** — An edge on the web: a connection to a graph or a web page, written in content or set on a node (`data.link`, `url`, `href`). It is drawn as an edge, by the `link` edge class, wherever both of its ends are in the workspace.\n- **ground** — What you stand on inside a graph: where you land when you go in. A graph with graph content has one; a document has none. Joined to the root by a `ground` edge from the root's `ground` port.\n- **faces** — How a graph or node looks from further away, largest to smallest. The author names them; the usual names are Detail, Summary and Icon. The first face is the page. What the app shows depends on a face's place in the order and the room it has, never on its name. The order ends at the Glyph. Stored as `face` nodes joined by `next-face` edges.\n- **Glyph** — A node's mark: the symbol that stands for it. Faces are how a node looks; the Glyph is how it is recognised. A graph has one because, seen from outside, a graph is a node. It is shown when there is no room for even the smallest face, and wherever the node is named in a small space: lists, the trail, link chips, tabs, bookmarks. It has its own editor and its name is always Glyph. Node type `glyph`.\n- **template** — A graph you start a new graph from. Templates are how the shapes that work get passed on, and each generation built from one can become a template in turn.\n- **workspace** — Where open graphs sit side by side. Not stored in graphs.",
        "memo": "Section of the Glossary. The Detail page contains the complete definitions; update both views when editing.",
        "showInBrowse": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-building",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Building",
      "position": {
        "x": 370,
        "y": -350
      },
      "width": 590,
      "height": 480,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary",
          "nodeId": "glossary-building"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#222b49,#171f38);border:2px solid #c9b8ff88;border-radius:18px;padding:5cqw;color:#f8f3ff;font-family:system-ui'><div style='color:#c9b8ff;font:800 3.4cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.16em'>03 / BUILDING</div><h2 style='margin:3cqw 0 2cqw;font:600 8cqw/.98 Georgia,serif'>The authoring language</h2><p style='margin:0 0 3cqw;font:400 3.2cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#bfcce7'>Structure, behavior, and reuse.</p><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>root</strong> (noun)</b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>The node a graph begins from. It says what the graph is (its name, description and faces) and everything else in the graph hangs from it. Every graph has exactly one. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>root</code>, in the file <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>root.node</code>.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>root</strong> (verb)</b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>To select several graphs in the workspace and make a new graph from them, with a root of its own. It is what Twilite has instead of grouping. It works on graphs only: lifting nodes out of a graph into a new one is copy and paste. <em>Not built yet.</em></span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Surface</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>One ordered set of faces a graph shows from outside. A graph can have more than one. A <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>surface</code> edge from the root to the first face.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Port</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>A named place where a graph offers something to other graphs. The root is the graph&#39;s root Port. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>port</code>; an edge&#39;s ends are its <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>sourcePort</code> and <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>targetPort</code>.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>export</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>What a graph offers to other graphs: a class, a set of classes, or the whole graph. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>export</code>.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>import</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>What a graph uses from another graph&#39;s export. A class used in a graph must come in through an import. Node type <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>import</code>.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Bridge</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>The connection from an export to the import that uses it: one graph drawing on another. A classed edge (the <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>bridge</code> edge class), drawn in the workspace between the two graphs, and what Tidy arranges by. Not stored: worked out from the imports and exports of open graphs.</span></div><div style='margin-top:15px;color:#c9b8ff;font:700 13px system-ui'>+ 8 more terms in the full Glossary page →</div></div>"
        },
        "sourceMarkdown": "Words for the template builder. A visitor never needs them.\n\n- **root** (noun) — The node a graph begins from. It says what the graph is (its name, description and faces) and everything else in the graph hangs from it. Every graph has exactly one. Node type `root`, in the file `root.node`.\n- **root** (verb) — To select several graphs in the workspace and make a new graph from them, with a root of its own. It is what Twilite has instead of grouping. It works on graphs only: lifting nodes out of a graph into a new one is copy and paste. *Not built yet.*\n- **Surface** — One ordered set of faces a graph shows from outside. A graph can have more than one. A `surface` edge from the root to the first face.\n- **Port** — A named place where a graph offers something to other graphs. The root is the graph's root Port. Node type `port`; an edge's ends are its `sourcePort` and `targetPort`.\n- **export** — What a graph offers to other graphs: a class, a set of classes, or the whole graph. Node type `export`.\n- **import** — What a graph uses from another graph's export. A class used in a graph must come in through an import. Node type `import`.\n- **Bridge** — The connection from an export to the import that uses it: one graph drawing on another. A classed edge (the `bridge` edge class), drawn in the workspace between the two graphs, and what Tidy arranges by. Not stored: worked out from the imports and exports of open graphs.\n- **class** — The definition a node or edge follows: which Properties it has and how it looks. On nodes and edges alike, `type` is the class key and `classRef` the class graph's address.\n- **Edge Class** — A class for edges: what a kind of connection means and what it may join. A graph built from the Edge Class template.\n- **Property** — One named value on a node, as its class defines it. Every Property announces `property.changed` when its value changes; nothing happens unless an edge is drawn from it. Node type `property`.\n- **Method** — The only code in a graph. It runs when something invokes it, and declares what it may change; the app refuses anything else. A Method has an `invokes` port of its own, so one Method can run the next. Node type `method`.\n- **invokes** — The edge that makes something happen: from what raises an event (a Property, a node, a button, a Method) to the Method it runs, naming the event. Every source draws its own edge: structure matters. Edge type `invokes`.\n- **Membrane** — A graph's boundary: what it holds in, and where its ports sit for things to cross. A graph grows from its root and is held by its membrane. Node type `membrane` for its control points, edge `membrane`, and the root's `data.membrane`.\n- **Specification** — The page that defines a primitive. An ordinary graph under `library/primitives/`.\n- **primitive** — One of the sixteen parts every graph is made of. Each has a Specification.",
        "memo": "Section of the Glossary. The Detail page contains the complete definitions; update both views when editing.",
        "showInBrowse": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-patterns",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Patterns",
      "position": {
        "x": -970,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 595.1376190029779,
      "height": 327.2476199404417,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary",
          "nodeId": "glossary-patterns"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#222b49,#171f38);border:2px solid #b4e9d488;border-radius:18px;padding:5cqw;color:#f8f3ff;font-family:system-ui'><div style='color:#b4e9d4;font:800 3.4cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.16em'>04 / PATTERNS</div><h2 style='margin:3cqw 0 2cqw;font:600 8cqw/.98 Georgia,serif'>Shapes, not categories</h2><p style='margin:0 0 3cqw;font:400 3.2cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#bfcce7'>One graph, many possibilities.</p></div>"
        },
        "sourceMarkdown": "A graph can grow into anything. Some shapes come up again and again: a page on its own, a picture, a list of doors to other graphs. People call these **documents**, **scenes** and **collections**. They are patterns, not kinds of graph: nothing in a file says which one a graph is, and a graph can be more than one at once. What a graph does follows from what is in it, never from a label on it (a graph with no ground shows its page because it has no ground). Templates are how the patterns are passed on.",
        "memo": "Section of the Glossary. The Detail page contains the complete definitions; update both views when editing.",
        "showInBrowse": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-in-the-app",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Navigation",
      "position": {
        "x": -300,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 590,
      "height": 430,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary",
          "nodeId": "glossary-in-the-app"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#222b49,#171f38);border:2px solid #f1c4d588;border-radius:18px;padding:5cqw;color:#f8f3ff;font-family:system-ui'><div style='color:#f1c4d5;font:800 3.4cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.16em'>05 / NAVIGATION</div><h2 style='margin:3cqw 0 2cqw;font:600 8cqw/.98 Georgia,serif'>Finding your way</h2><p style='margin:0 0 3cqw;font:400 3.2cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#bfcce7'>The language of moving through the Zone.</p><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#f1c4d5;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>inside</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>Where you are after entering a graph: standing on its ground.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#f1c4d5;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>trail</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>The path at the top of the window: Workspace › graph › node › Inside.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#f1c4d5;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>going through</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>Following a Portal, or the end of a line, to where it leads.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#f1c4d5;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Tidy</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>Arranging the workspace: graphs joined by a Bridge close together (imports on the left, exports on the right), Portal graphs further out.</span></div></div>"
        },
        "sourceMarkdown": "Words for finding your way.\n\n- **inside** — Where you are after entering a graph: standing on its ground.\n- **trail** — The path at the top of the window: Workspace › graph › node › Inside.\n- **going through** — Following a Portal, or the end of a line, to where it leads.\n- **Tidy** — Arranging the workspace: graphs joined by a Bridge close together (imports on the left, exports on the right), Portal graphs further out.",
        "memo": "Section of the Glossary. The Detail page contains the complete definitions; update both views when editing.",
        "showInBrowse": true
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "glossary-retired",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Older words",
      "position": {
        "x": 370,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 590,
      "height": 430,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 0
        }
      ],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "glossary",
          "nodeId": "glossary-retired"
        },
        "content": {
          "kind": "html",
          "value": "<div style='container-type:inline-size;width:100%;height:100%;box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;background:linear-gradient(145deg,#222b49,#171f38);border:2px solid #d0d4e388;border-radius:18px;padding:5cqw;color:#f8f3ff;font-family:system-ui'><div style='color:#d0d4e3;font:800 3.4cqw system-ui;letter-spacing:.16em'>06 / OLDER WORDS</div><h2 style='margin:3cqw 0 2cqw;font:600 8cqw/.98 Georgia,serif'>What terminology replaced</h2><p style='margin:0 0 3cqw;font:400 3.2cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#bfcce7'>A map from older vocabulary to the current model.</p><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Declaration</strong>, <strong>manifest</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>the root.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>View</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>a face. <strong>View sequence</strong> — next-face.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>landing surface</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>the ground.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Field</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>a Property.</span></div><div style='border-top:1px solid #48516b;padding:2cqw 0'><b style='display:block;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 3.9cqw/1.2 system-ui'><strong>Markdown node</strong></b><span style='font:400 3.0cqw/1.42 system-ui;color:#d0d8e9'>Content with kind <code style='color:#e6c987;background:#242c48;border-radius:4px;padding:0 3px'>markdown</code>.</span></div><div style='margin-top:15px;color:#d0d4e3;font:700 13px system-ui'>+ 6 more terms in the full Glossary page →</div></div>"
        },
        "sourceMarkdown": "Words older graphs and pages use, and what replaced them. A current graph never uses them.\n\n- **Declaration**, **manifest** — the root.\n- **View** — a face. **View sequence** — next-face.\n- **landing surface** — the ground.\n- **Field** — a Property.\n- **Markdown node** — Content with kind `markdown`.\n- **Bridge node** — an import or an export. The Bridge is the connection between them.\n- **Graph Reference** — a Portal.\n- **groups** — rooting.\n- **frame**, **border**, **wrap** (for a graph's boundary) — the Membrane.\n- **handle** — An editor interaction point on a node. A Port is a named connection interface; the modern model distinguishes ports from handles.\n- **Event Handler**, **Script** — leaving in favour of Methods and the `invokes` edge.",
        "memo": "Section of the Glossary. The Detail page contains the complete definitions; update both views when editing.",
        "showInBrowse": true
      }
    },
